From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: mux: ps883x: reconcile the retimer after a hibernation restore

One of the three PS8830 retimers comes back powered and fully configured after
a cross-boot resume, and stays that way until reboot:

    $ sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/regmap/3-0008/registers
    00: 21      CONN_STATUS_0: CONNECTION_PRESENT | USB_3_1_CONNECTED
    01: 85      CONN_STATUS_1: DP_CONNECTED | DP_PIN_ASSIGNMENT_C_D | DP_HPD_LEVEL
    02: 00

A USB 3.1 SuperSpeed path and a four-lane DisplayPort path with HPD asserted,
into a port with nothing plugged into it, on battery. The other two read back
as not responding, which is correct. Persistent: byte-identical five minutes
later, and it does not self-heal.

The driver has no dev_pm_ops at all, and tracks its entire power state in
retimer->in_reset. That bool comes back out of the image saying the chip is
held in reset with its rails down; the pads that back it do not come back at
all. On this board every control line of that one retimer is a PMIC GPIO --

    reset-gpios  = <&pm8550_gpios 10>        (ps8830 node)
    vdd/vddar/vddat enable = <&pmc8380_5_gpios 8>
    vddio enable = <&pm8550ve_9_gpios 8>
    vdd33/vdd33-cap enable = <&pm8550_gpios 11>

-- while all six lines of the other two are TLMM. pinctrl-msm replays its pad
state across a restore; nothing replays pinctrl-spmi-gpio. So exactly the
retimer whose pads are not replayed is the one that comes back live, which is
what picks this out from an ordinary regulator-refcount bug.

Nothing ever corrects it. ps883x_retimer_probe() is what normally drives these
pads into agreement with in_reset -- it ends in an unconditional ps883x_reset()
-- and probe() does not run on a restore. A cold boot is clean for exactly that
reason.

Add a .restore that drives the rails and the reset line once through the
framework, so the pads match what the driver already believes. It goes through
regulator_enable() rather than writing the enable GPIOs directly because that is
what keeps the use counts balanced: they are already zero at this point, so a
bare ps883x_disable_vregs() would underflow them.

Only .restore, deliberately: .thaw runs on the hibernating kernel with the
hardware untouched, where this reconciliation is not merely unnecessary but
describes a power cycle that did not happen. Aliasing the two is how several
restore callbacks in this tree corrupt live state under /sys/power/pm_test.

Only the in_reset case is handled. A retimer that was configured when the image
was taken comes back equally wrong in the other direction, but replaying that
needs the last cfg values cached and cannot be tested here without a dock; the
next Type-C notification reconfigures it.

While here, fix an unrelated unbalanced disable on the unbind path.
ps883x_retimer_remove() calls ps883x_disable_vregs() unconditionally, so
unbinding a retimer that is in reset -- the resting state probe() leaves behind
-- underflows all six regulators:

    unbalanced disables for VREG_RTMR0_1P8
     ps883x_retimer_remove+0x54/0xb0 [ps883x]
    ... x6

ps883x_reset() already does exactly what those two statements do, and is a no-op
when the retimer is already in reset.

Measured on a Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (X1E-80-100), awake idle with the panel off,
one hibernate cycle into the boot: 2400 mW before, 1640 mW after driving the
pads back by hand with an unbind/bind. Clean-boot baseline on the same machine
is 1140-1240 mW.

--- a/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/usb/pd.h>
@@ -501,16 +502,62 @@
 	typec_retimer_unregister(retimer->retimer);
 	typec_switch_unregister(retimer->sw);
 
-	gpiod_set_value(retimer->reset_gpio, 1);
+	ps883x_reset(retimer);
 
 	clk_disable_unprepare(retimer->xo_clk);
 
-	ps883x_disable_vregs(retimer);
-
 	typec_mux_put(retimer->typec_mux);
 	typec_switch_put(retimer->typec_switch);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A hibernation image carries retimer->in_reset, but the pads that back it do
+ * not survive the power cycle. On the Yoga Slim 7x every control line of one of
+ * the three retimers -- reset and all three regulator enables -- is a PMIC GPIO,
+ * and nothing replays pinctrl-spmi-gpio state across a restore. That chip comes
+ * back powered and still carrying its previous configuration while the driver
+ * believes it is held in reset, and nothing corrects it: probe() is what
+ * normally drives these pads to the state in_reset describes, and probe() does
+ * not run on a restore.
+ *
+ * Observed: CONN_STATUS_0 = 0x21, CONN_STATUS_1 = 0x85 -- USB 3.1 plus four-lane
+ * DP with HPD asserted, into a port with nothing in it, for the rest of the boot.
+ *
+ * Drive the rails and the reset line once through the framework so the pads
+ * match what the driver believes. Going through regulator_enable() rather than
+ * writing the enable GPIOs directly is what keeps the use counts balanced: they
+ * are already zero here, so a bare ps883x_disable_vregs() would underflow.
+ *
+ * Only the in_reset case is handled. A retimer that was *configured* when the
+ * image was taken comes back equally wrong in the other direction, but replaying
+ * that needs the last cfg values cached and cannot be tested without a dock; the
+ * next Type-C notification reconfigures it.
+ */
+static int ps883x_retimer_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct ps883x_retimer *retimer = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&retimer->lock);
+
+	if (!retimer->in_reset)
+		return 0;
+
+	ret = ps883x_enable_vregs(retimer);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* ps883x_reset() is a no-op while in_reset, and the pads are the point */
+	retimer->in_reset = false;
+	ps883x_reset(retimer);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct dev_pm_ops ps883x_retimer_pm_ops = {
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(ps883x_retimer_restore),
+};
+
 static const struct of_device_id ps883x_retimer_of_table[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "parade,ps8830" },
 	{ }
@@ -521,6 +568,7 @@
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "ps883x_retimer",
 		.of_match_table = ps883x_retimer_of_table,
+		.pm = pm_ptr(&ps883x_retimer_pm_ops),
 	},
 	.probe		= ps883x_retimer_probe,
 	.remove		= ps883x_retimer_remove,
